course: dictionary
lesson: 3762
title: Foam: bubbles and soft stuff
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Foam is a mass of many tiny bubbles. Context helps you spot it: «Washing-up liquid in a model volcano traps the carbon dioxide in bubbles and makes foam.» The clue is bubbles, so foam is the bubbly stuff that forms, not the liquid itself.
explain: Foam can also be a soft material full of tiny holes. «Sand, mats and the foam in a helmet soften a blow by letting you stop more slowly.» Here foam is not a bubbly liquid. It is a squashy material that gives way when you hit it.
explain: Foam can be a verb too: to make or be covered with bubbles. Be careful with «foaming at the mouth». In everyday talk it means very angry, but if an animal is foaming at the mouth, it may be very ill. Keep away and tell an adult.
fact: Foam is a mixture of gas trapped in a liquid or a solid.
fact: Soap foams because it traps air in thin films.
fact: Foam rubber is soft and light, so it is used for cushions, mattresses and toys.
fact: Polystyrene foam insulates heat, which is why it is used for hot-drink cups, but it is hard to recycle.
fact: «Foam» can be a noun for bubbles or a material, and «foaming» is the verb for making bubbles.
exercise: «Washing-up liquid in a model volcano traps the carbon dioxide in bubbles and makes foam.» What is the foam here? | *A mass of tiny bubbles | A hard, heavy rock | A kind of metal | A cold, dry powder
exercise: What is foam rubber used for? | Making the sea salty | Growing plants in a garden | Cooking eggs in a pan | *Cushions and mattresses
exercise: Which word means to make or be covered with bubbles? | Folding | Freezing | *Foaming | Floating
test: «A bike helmet's foam crushes in a crash so the brain is shaken less.» Why is foam useful in a helmet? | It makes the helmet float on water | It makes the helmet lighter to carry | *It crushes to soften a knock | It keeps the head warm in winter
test: Why does soap make foam? | It dissolves the sand | *It traps air in thin films | It turns into oxygen | It freezes the water
exam: «Foam is just lots of bubbles; soap particles do the cleaning whether there is foam or not.» What is true? | Cleaning only happens when there is foam | Foam does the cleaning, not the soap | *Foam is bubbles, and the soap particles do the cleaning | Foam is always dangerous
